# `module::Field::validate`

[Back to `module::Field`](../Field.md)

Checks the whole field and throws, listing every problem, if it cannot be priced.

## Signature

```cpp
void module::Field::validate() const;
```

## What It Does

`validate` runs the checks that `price` runs first, without pricing: every member must be defined, finite and in its domain (positive strikes, volatilities and mean-reversion speeds, a correlation in (−1, 1), non-negative maturities and so on); strike, maturity, spot, rate and yield must each have one element or the same number; the engine must support the model and the exercise style; and the engine's own settings must be sensible (positive path counts, enough lattice steps). All problems are reported together, each naming its member, so a field assembled by hand can be corrected in one pass. A model that is legal but suspicious (a Heston parameter set that violates the Feller condition) is accepted.

Call it after building or editing a field when you want a clear failure before an expensive pricing or calibration.

## Returns

Nothing; throws on error.

## Errors

- An invalid field throws (`pluss::Error`) listing every problem.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

A sound field passes silently.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.05, 0.02),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::BlackScholes, {0.2}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic, .risk = mod::Risk::All}};

  field.validate();
  out.writeln("valid");
}
```

### Example 2

A mismatch of lengths. Three strikes and two maturities cannot be paired; the problem is named.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = {.type = fin::pricing::Call, .strike = pluss::make_series({90.0, 100.0, 110.0}), .maturity = pluss::make_series({0.5, 1.0})},
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::BlackScholes, {0.2}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic}};
  try {
    (void)(field.validate());
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find("Triggered")));
  }
}
```

### Example 3

An engine that does not support the model is caught before pricing; so is a setting of the engine in use that is out of range.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Lattice, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};

  try {
    (void)(field.validate());
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find("Triggered")));
  }

  field.controls.engine = mod::Engine::MonteCarlo;
  field.controls.mc.paths = -1;
  try {
    (void)(field.validate());
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find("Triggered")));
  }
}
```

### Example 4

A legal but suspicious model does not stop validation. This Heston set violates the Feller condition `2κθ ≥ σ²` (the variance can reach zero), yet the field validates and prices.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {0.5, 0.02, 0.8, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Cos, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};
  field.validate();
  out.writeln("valid; price =", field.price().price.items<double>());
}
```

